==German==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|de|goh|stelan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*stelan}}. Compare {{cog|is|stela}},  {{cog|nds|stehlen}}, {{cog|nl|stelen}}, {{cog|en|steal}}, {{cog|da|stjæle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈʃteːlən/}}
* {{audio|de|De-stehlen.ogg|audio}}
* {{hyphenation|de|steh|len}}
* {{homophones|de|Stelen}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|stehlen<stiehlt#stahl,gestohlen,stähle:stöhle>}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[steal]]
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to [[skulk]], to [[move]] [[secretly]]

====Usage notes====
* The person from whom the thing is stolen is given in the dative case. Thus, {{m|de||Er hat '''mir''' ein Buch gestohlen}} means: “He stole a book from me.” This is quite contrary to English usage, where “He stole me a book” would be the equivalent of German: {{m|de||Er hat ein Buch '''für''' mich gestohlen.}} The person ''for'' whom the thing is stolen must be expressed as an object of "für".
* An exception to this, however, is a [[reflexive]] dative, which will have the same sense as in English: {{m|de||Er hat '''sich''' ein Buch gestohlen}} means: “He stole himself a book.” This exception is implied by logic, since one cannot steal from oneself.

