==Low German==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|nds|gem-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|nds|osx|stelan}}, from {{inh|nds|gmw-pro|*stelan}}.

===Verb===
{{head|nds|verb|third-person singular simple present|stehlt|past tense|stohl|past participle|stahlen|auxiliary verb|hebben}}

# {{lb|nds|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[steal]]
# {{lb|nds|reflexive}} to [[skulk]], to [[move]] [[secretly]]

====Usage notes====
As in High German, the person from whom the thing is stolen is given in the oblique case. Thus, {{m|nds||He hett '''mi''' en Book stahlen}} means: “He stole a book from me.” This is quite contrary to English usage, where “He stole me a book” would be the equivalent of Low German: {{m|nds||He hett en Book '''för''' mi stahlen.}}

